Fix a one-byte heap overflow in stat's format handling.
print_it() copies each directive's "%" and modifiers into a buffer
sized strlen(format)+3, on the assumption that the conversion letter
is replaced by at most "llu". Where LL_FMT is "I64", as with msvcrt,
the 64-bit conversions append "I64u", "I64x" or "I64d", one byte more,
so a format that is a single directive, such as "stat -c %s", wrote
the terminating NUL one byte past the end of the buffer. Size the
buffer from LL_FMT.

---
 coreutils/stat.c |    6 +++---
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/coreutils/stat.c
+++ b/coreutils/stat.c
@@ -443,9 +443,9 @@
 {
 	/* Create a working copy of the format string */
 	char *format = xstrdup(masterformat);
-	/* Add 2 to accommodate our conversion of the stat '%s' format string
-	 * to the printf '%llu' one.  */
-	char *dest = xmalloc(strlen(format) + 2 + 1);
+	/* Leave room for our conversion of the stat '%s' format string
+	 * to the printf '%llu' one (LL_FMT may be longer than "ll").  */
+	char *dest = xmalloc(strlen(format) + sizeof(LL_FMT"u"));
 	char *b;
 
 	b = format;
